## Security gate

This repository has a security gate. Run it before you say the work is done:

    {{VALIDATE_COMMAND}}

Exit 0 means nothing is blocking. Exit 1 means something is. Exit 3 means part of the gate could
not run, which is not the same as passing.

**A refusal is not a pass.** If a tool refuses, errors, or reports that it did not run, that check
produced no evidence about this project. Say so plainly in your summary. Do not describe it as
passing and do not count it toward "all checks green".

**Tests are evidence only when they run and reach the code.** A suite that is never executed, that
imports nothing from this project, or that asserts something true by construction is not evidence
that anything works — however many assertions it contains and whether or not it passes. Install the
dependencies and run it.

**A written justification only covers what it names.** If you argue that findings do not apply, say
which findings. An argument about one package does not carry to another, and the gate reports which
findings are in packages this project depends on directly.

**If a finding genuinely does not apply, say so where the gate will record it.** Some rules cannot
fit every project: a rule requiring a workload to prove an identity rather than hold a shared secret
has no remedy to offer for an HMAC secret shared with a third party, because there is nothing to
replace it with. In a Kubernetes manifest, write the opt-out as a comment naming the rule from the
finding and why it does not apply here:

    # identity-guard:allow identity.secret-as-auth  inbound HMAC from the provider, no identity to prove

Every allowance is reported in the gate output with its reason, so this records the decision rather
than removing the finding. Prose in a document the gate does not read is not an opt-out. Use it only
where the rule cannot apply, never where the fix is merely inconvenient — a reason that is not true
of this project is worse than the finding it hides.

**End your summary with what the gate did not establish.** Before anything else you say about the
result, list every check that reported "did NOT run", "NOT EXECUTED", or "not checked", and every
blocking finding you are leaving. Exit 0 means nothing is blocking — not that everything was
checked, and not that what was checked was verified end to end.

Do not describe the work as complete, production-ready, or fully validated unless the gate ran every
check and all of them passed. If some checks could not run, the accurate sentence names them: "the
gate reports no blocking findings; the test suite could not be executed here, so nothing establishes
that it passes." A reader who is not looking at the gate output has only your summary to go on.
